review_comments.body:
the update looks good and solves the more severe case of stale props (basically capturing it on first use and never updating it looks like) but I was actually more thinking of this edge case

Imagine this component
```
function MyComponent({ someKey }) {
  // update when name changes from dynamically referenced context property
  let ctx = useContext(MyContext, (next, prev) => { next[someKey].name !== prev[someKey] .name})
  return ... // use ctx[someKey].name presumably
}
```

```
let things = {
  aThing: { name: 'foo' }
};

//render
<MyContext.Provider value={things}>
  <MyComponent someKey={Object.keys(things)[0]} /> //get the first thing key
</MyContext.Provider>

// then change things
things = {
  aDifferentThing: { name: 'bar' }
};

//render again
// when Provider notifies subs the captured shouldUpdate will do `things[aThing].name` and will throw
<MyContext.Provider value={things}>
  <MyComponent someKey={Object.keys(things)[0]} />
</MyContext.Provider>
```

