review_comments.body:
That's a great start! Lookig more closely at the implementation in React it seems like the `context` arg is passed as a `thisArgument` and not passed as a "standard" argument in the classic sense:

```js
mapIntoArray(children, result, '', '', function(child) {
    return func.call(context, child, count++);
    //          ^^^^
});
```

According to [MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Function/call) the signature of `Function.prototype.call()` is:

```js
func.call([thisArg[, arg1, arg2, ...argN]])
```

This means that the callback function will keep the same signature as before, so there should be no failing tests from this change.

```js
Children.map(children, (child, index) => ...);
// instead of 
Children.map(children, (context, child, index) => ...);
```

 We'd only need a new one asserting that the `this` value inside the callback function is the same as  `context`.

